## Command/tool reachability audit committed

Repository-visible audit:

- `docs/product/ARQ-UI-UX-COMMAND-TOOL-REACHABILITY-AUDIT.md`
- commit `48c5a7399166a6288a77b76a374b5b660ae471a6`
- audited from `main` @ `028a5125aca41da7069af805be8515a6a2776497`

### Key finding
The repository currently has **54 designed tool IDs**, **11 module-backed IDs**, but only **4 active-tool IDs with a clearly identified current Plan product consumer**: `select`, `wall`, `pan`, `fit`.

Seven IDs are module-backed but product active-tool reachability is not proven and can currently be enabled by the rail because availability uses repository backing as its proxy: `window-select`, `crossing-select`, `selection-filter`, `door`, `window`, `room-boundary`, and `zoom` as an armed tool ID. This is exactly the library-vs-product distinction this issue was intended to expose.

Other concrete drift found:
- palette metadata is a local `COMMAND_ENTRIES` list rather than shared command metadata;
- Door and Room can be palette-enabled even though the audited Plan active-tool contract does not consume those IDs;
- `close-tab` lacks a palette disabled reason when the active tab is not closeable;
- `export-dxf` is a permanently placeholder-disabled palette entry in this path;
- keyboard registry declares `save` but App has no Cmd/Ctrl+S handler;
- Windows redo advertises Ctrl+Y but App handles only Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+Z;
- `focus-selection` is registered but no dedicated handler was found;
- desktop Share is an enabled button whose host callback only records a demo action, while phone Share is honestly capability-disabled;
- desktop Account likewise records a demo action rather than proving a real account menu.

### Status
I am not closing #398 yet. Static classification is complete, but the issue still requires exact-head registry validation and representative real-browser evidence before closure. It also needs a final refresh if active App wiring changes before the gate.

No runtime command/tool code was changed by this audit.

## Capability-ledger input from UI/UX reachability audit

#398 has produced `docs/product/ARQ-UI-UX-COMMAND-TOOL-REACHABILITY-AUDIT.md` at commit `48c5a7399166a6288a77b76a374b5b660ae471a6`.

The important evidence distinction for #370 is:
- 54 designed tool IDs;
- 11 IDs have repository backing;
- only `select`, `wall`, `pan`, `fit` currently have a clearly identified active-tool consumer in the audited Plan product path;
- `door`, `window`, `room-boundary`, `window-select`, `crossing-select`, `selection-filter` and `zoom` as an armed tool ID are module-backed but should not be promoted to `verified_current` from that fact alone;
- current zoom interaction via wheel/pinch is real, but that does not prove the registry `zoom` tool command itself is product-reachable;
- all remaining tool IDs should stay designed/planned/capability-gated according to stronger evidence.

Please consume this as source evidence when seeding tool-related capability states. Do not copy the classification without rechecking the exact integration head at ledger generation time.

